{# What the site tells the apps, and the one way it tells them.

   The apps -- oeee-cafe-apple (iOS and macOS), oeee-cafe-android, and
   oeee-cafe-desktop (Windows, on Steam and the Microsoft Store) -- are web
   views around this site. Each used to learn what it needed by reading the
   page: a CSS class on the unread badge, the painter's paths, which images
   were drawings, a pixel under the status bar. Every one of those broke the
   day the site changed and nothing said so; the unread badge on Windows and
   macOS read 0 for weeks after the bell stopped rendering `.toolbar-badge`.
   So the site says these things itself, and the apps listen.

   The channel is one object called `oeeeBridge`, which each app registers
   before the page's first script runs:

     iOS, macOS   webkit.messageHandlers.oeeeBridge (WKScriptMessageHandler)
     Android      window.oeeeBridge (WebViewCompat.addWebMessageListener,
                  restricted to the site's origin)
     Windows      window.oeeeBridge, an init script whose postMessage emits
                  the Tauri event `oeee-bridge` with the string as payload

   Every message is a JSON string -- a string because that is all Android's
   listener carries -- of an object with `v` (this contract's version, 1) and
   `type`. The apps ignore types they do not know and fields they do not
   know, so the site can add either without a release of any of them.

   page     {path, signedIn, presence, community, group, painting, refreshable}
            On every page, and again whenever any of it changes: a boosted
            navigation, a back swipe that restored the page. `signedIn` is
            null on a page without the toolbar, which cannot tell. `presence`
            is presence_meta.jinja's activity, or null for browsing;
            `painting` is whether leaving would lose a drawing in progress,
            and `refreshable` whether pulling down may reload the page --
            neither a painter nor a replay may be.
   unread   {count}
            The number on the bell (nav_notifications.jinja): unread
            notifications and invitations waiting, together. Sent whenever it
            changes, which is when a request swaps the bell.
   theme    {choice, dark, top, bottom, ground, grid}
            `choice` is "light", "dark" or "system"; `dark` what that comes
            to now. `top` and `bottom` are the colours at the page's two
            edges, as CSS colours, for the bars an app draws against them --
            or null where nothing there has a colour, the app's own then.
            `ground` and `grid` are the design system's --ds-ground and
            --ds-grid (ds.css): the field behind every page and the lines
            ruled on it every 14px, for an app that draws the same field
            where the page does not reach -- under an overscroll, behind the
            window before the page arrives -- and would otherwise have to
            keep its own copy of NEO's colours in step with this one.
   words    {leaveTitle, leaveBody, leave, stay, ok, cancel, saveImage,
             copyImage, share, copyLink, savedImage, savedFile, saveFailed,
             steamSignInFailed}
            What an app says in dialogs and menus of its own over the page
            -- the leave prompt, the long press on a drawing, the right-click
            menu -- in the page's language, from the site's catalogues
            (locales/*.ftl, the app-* messages). Sent once a page, since the
            reader can change language between pages. An app keeps words of
            its own only for what it says with no page to speak for it: a
            site that cannot be reached, the tabs before the first page.
   haptic   {name}
            "light", "medium", "selection", "success", "warning" or "error":
            a control that is felt as well as seen.
   pressed  {drawing: {src, link, width, height} | null}
            As a finger lands, whether it is on a drawing -- an image marked
            data-oeee-drawing, which is never a sensitive one -- so an app
            that opens its own menu on a long press knows which in time.
            `link` is the post's page, or "" on the post page itself.
   painter  {state: "ready"}
            The painter can be driven: oeeeApp.painter.command(name) and
            .preferPen() (frontend/shared/appBridge.ts).
   prices   {store, products}
            What these cost, asked as /supporter opens: `products` are the
            App Store product ids or Steam app ids on the page. The app
            answers by calling oeeeApp.storePrices({product: price}) with
            the store's own formatted strings -- StoreKit's `displayPrice`,
            Steam's `price_overview.final_formatted` -- in the reader's
            currency, which only the store knows. It may call it again when
            it knows better, and a page whose app never answers shows the
            button without a price.
   purchase {store, product, year}
            Sell this year's Supporter Pack, pressed on /supporter:
            `store` is "apple" or "steam", `product` the App Store product
            id or the Steam app id of that year's pack. The app sells it and
            posts what it got back to the site --
            /auth/apple/purchase with the StoreKit transaction id, or
            /auth/steam/refresh with a Web API ticket -- and reloads the
            page when the site answers. Nothing the page says records a
            purchase; the site asks the store itself (src/app_store.rs,
            src/steam.rs).
   restore  {}
            The same, for a purchase already made: StoreKit hands the
            transaction over again and the app posts it to the same place.
            It gives a pack back to whoever is signed in and never makes a
            second one. Steam needs none of this -- it says what an account
            owns every time it is asked.
   signIn   {provider, nonce}
   browse   {url}
            A sign-in the page is carrying for an app that stopped the link
            to /auth/apple or /auth/google: make an ID token for `nonce`
            with the platform's own sheet, or open this site's `url` in the
            system's browser (app_sign_in.jinja).
   share    {title, text}
   download {name, mime, data}
            What Android's web view cannot do itself (app_polyfills.jinja).

   The apps are told apart by what each adds to its web view's user agent,
   in one place (theme_head.jinja), which marks the root before the page
   paints: `data-app="ios"` or `"android"` for the phone apps, whose pages
   scroll whole under a native tab bar, and `data-desktop="macos"` or
   `"windows"` for the desktop windows, whose toolbar is the title bar. The
   site owns what those marks change, the room kept for a window's own
   controls included (ds.css); an app says what it is and nothing about the
   site's layout.

   window.oeeeApp.wouldLoseWork() is whether leaving now would lose
   something: the page's own `beforeunload` handlers, asked the way a
   browser asks them, which neither WKWebView nor a window being closed
   asks by itself. The painter answers only for a drawing with something in
   it, and not while it is being saved -- which `painting` above cannot say.

   A build that can sell says so by marking the root element
   `data-store="apple"` or `data-store="steam"` before the page paints --
   the same way the Steam app already marks it `data-steam-app` for the
   Steam sign-in button, and early enough that the heart in the toolbar and
   the buttons on /supporter are never drawn in a build that would do
   nothing with them. It is the build that says so, not the platform: an
   older iOS app has no StoreKit in it, and the Microsoft Store build of the
   desktop app is not the Steam one. A build that says nothing is offered
   nothing to press.

   The other way, the apps call the page, and everything they call is on
   one object, window.oeeeApp, which this template makes. The parts of the
   page that answer an app add themselves to it as they load, and a page
   without them simply lacks the member, so an app asks for one it may not
   find as `window.oeeeApp && oeeeApp.command && oeeeApp.command(name)`:

     wouldLoseWork()         here; see below
     signIn.*                app_sign_in.jinja
     command(name)           toolbar.jinja: the site's menu commands and keys,
                             true when the page knew the name
     restoreContent()        toolbar.jinja: takes the loading skeleton back
                             down after a link the app stopped
     storePrices(prices)     supporter.jinja
     painter.command(name), painter.preferPen()
                             frontend/shared/appBridge.ts, on a drawing page

   And --oeee-text-scale on the root, which is CSS rather than a call.

   The Mac app's own window chrome (MacWindow.js in oeee-cafe-apple) posts
   on the same channel -- {v:1, type:"window", action:"drag"|"zoom"} when
   the toolbar's empty space is pressed -- so every app has one channel to
   listen on; nothing in the site sends it.

   There is no other channel. The web-view apps were never released before
   this contract; the builds people have from before it are the native
   apps, which never load these pages. #}
<script>
  (function () {
    var root = document.documentElement;
    function channel() {
      var handlers = window.webkit && window.webkit.messageHandlers;
      var target = (handlers && handlers.oeeeBridge) || window.oeeeBridge;
      return target && target.postMessage ? target : null;
    }
    var last = {};
    // Sends a message of `type`, unless it is the same as the last one of
    // that type and `always` is not set. Returns whether an app heard it.
    function post(type, data, always) {
      var target = channel();
      if (!target) return false;
      // The envelope is written last, so a field of the message's own that
      // happens to share its name -- a file's MIME type, say -- can never
      // make it another kind of message.
      var message = {};
      for (var key in data) message[key] = data[key];
      message.v = 1;
      message.type = type;
      var text = JSON.stringify(message);
      // Only what is compared is kept: a download can be megabytes.
      if (!always) {
        if (last[type] === text) return true;
        last[type] = text;
      }
      try {
        target.postMessage(text);
      } catch (error) {
        return false;
      }
      return true;
    }

    // A boosted navigation keeps the head it arrived in (toolbar.jinja), so
    // the presence meta a painter page was loaded with is still there on the
    // page after it. Boosted links only ever lead to pages without one.
    var boosted = false;
    var PAINTING = /^(drawing|relaying|drawing-banner|collaborating)$/;
    function pageState() {
      var meta = boosted ? null : document.querySelector('meta[name="oeee-presence"]');
      var presence = meta ? meta.getAttribute("content") : null;
      var bar = document.querySelector(".nav-bar");
      return {
        path: location.pathname,
        signedIn: bar ? bar.hasAttribute("data-signed-in") : null,
        presence: presence,
        community: (meta && meta.getAttribute("data-community")) || null,
        group: (meta && meta.getAttribute("data-group")) || null,
        painting: PAINTING.test(presence || ""),
        refreshable: !presence
      };
    }

    // A colour someone can see: the first background up from `element`, the
    // root's included, that is not transparent. Null when there is none --
    // not transparent black, which an app would paint as black.
    function colourAt(element) {
      for (; element && element.nodeType === 1; element = element.parentElement) {
        var colour = getComputedStyle(element).backgroundColor;
        if (colour && colour !== "transparent" && !/^rgba\(.*,\s*0\)$/.test(colour)) return colour;
      }
      return null;
    }
    function themeState() {
      var choice = root.getAttribute("data-theme") || "system";
      var system = window.matchMedia && window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)").matches;
      return {
        choice: choice,
        dark: choice === "dark" || (choice === "system" && !!system),
        top: document.body ? colourAt(document.querySelector(".nav-bar") || document.body) : null,
        bottom: document.body ? colourAt(document.body) : null,
        ground: token("--ds-ground"),
        grid: token("--ds-grid")
      };
    }
    // A design-system colour as the root resolves it now, theme included;
    // null on a page without the design system's stylesheet.
    function token(name) {
      var value = getComputedStyle(root).getPropertyValue(name);
      return value ? value.trim() || null : null;
    }

    var WORDS = {
      leaveTitle: {{ ftl_get_message("app-leave-title")|tojson }},
      leaveBody: {{ ftl_get_message("app-leave-body")|tojson }},
      leave: {{ ftl_get_message("app-leave")|tojson }},
      stay: {{ ftl_get_message("app-stay")|tojson }},
      ok: {{ ftl_get_message("app-ok")|tojson }},
      cancel: {{ ftl_get_message("app-cancel")|tojson }},
      saveImage: {{ ftl_get_message("app-save-image")|tojson }},
      copyImage: {{ ftl_get_message("app-copy-image")|tojson }},
      share: {{ ftl_get_message("app-share")|tojson }},
      copyLink: {{ ftl_get_message("app-copy-link")|tojson }},
      savedImage: {{ ftl_get_message("app-saved-image")|tojson }},
      savedFile: {{ ftl_get_message("app-saved-file")|tojson }},
      saveFailed: {{ ftl_get_message("app-save-failed")|tojson }},
      steamSignInFailed: {{ ftl_get_message("app-steam-sign-in-failed")|tojson }}
    };

    function report() {
      if (!channel()) return;
      post("words", WORDS);
      post("page", pageState());
      post("theme", themeState());
      var bell = document.getElementById("nav-notifications");
      if (bell) post("unread", { count: parseInt(bell.getAttribute("data-unread"), 10) || 0 });
    }

    document.addEventListener("DOMContentLoaded", report);
    window.addEventListener("load", report);
    window.addEventListener("pageshow", function (event) {
      if (event.persisted) boosted = false;
      report();
    });
    window.addEventListener("popstate", function () { setTimeout(report, 0); });
    document.addEventListener("htmx:after:swap", function (event) {
      var ctx = event.detail && event.detail.ctx;
      if (event.target === document.body || (ctx && ctx.target === document.body)) boosted = true;
      report();
    });
    if (window.MutationObserver) {
      new MutationObserver(report).observe(root, { attributes: true, attributeFilter: ["data-theme"] });
    }
    if (window.matchMedia) {
      var scheme = window.matchMedia("(prefers-color-scheme: dark)");
      if (scheme.addEventListener) scheme.addEventListener("change", report);
      else if (scheme.addListener) scheme.addListener(report);
    }

    // Which drawing a finger lands on, said as it lands: by the time a long
    // press is recognised the app can no longer ask the page in time.
    window.addEventListener("touchstart", function (event) {
      if (!channel()) return;
      var target = event.target;
      var image = target && target.closest ? target.closest("img[data-oeee-drawing]") : null;
      var link = image ? image.closest("a[href]") : null;
      post("pressed", {
        drawing: image ? {
          src: image.currentSrc || image.src,
          link: link ? link.href : "",
          width: image.naturalWidth || image.width,
          height: image.naturalHeight || image.height
        } : null
      }, true);
    }, { capture: true, passive: true });

    window.oeeeApp = {
      // Whether an app is listening at all.
      connected: function () { return !!channel(); },
      post: post,
      // A control felt as well as seen.
      feel: function (name) {
        if (name) post("haptic", { name: name }, true);
      },
      // Said again after something the page changed by itself.
      report: report,
      // Whether the page would stop a browser from leaving it. Its value is
      // the answer, so an app evaluates the call and reads it.
      wouldLoseWork: function () {
        var event;
        try {
          event = document.createEvent("BeforeUnloadEvent");
          event.initEvent("beforeunload", false, true);
        } catch (error) {
          event = new Event("beforeunload", { cancelable: true });
        }
        window.dispatchEvent(event);
        return event.defaultPrevented ||
          (typeof event.returnValue === "string" && event.returnValue !== "");
      }
    };
  })();
</script>
